    'body'        => <<<'HTML'
<p class="lead">A public adjuster salary is not really a salary at all. Public adjusters (PAs) are paid a percentage of the insurance settlements they win for policyholders, so their income rises and falls with the number of clients they sign, the size of those claims and the fee caps in the states where they work.</p>

<div class="callout"><strong>Quick answer:</strong> Most public adjusters earn about $50,000 to $100,000 a year, and established PAs with steady commercial or large-loss work can earn $150,000 or more. They are paid a contingency fee, typically 10% to 20% of the settlement, and many states cap that percentage, including Texas at 10%.</div>

<h2 id="what-public-adjusters-do">What public adjusters do and who pays them</h2>
<p>Staff and independent adjusters work for insurance companies. Public adjusters work for the policyholder. A homeowner or business owner hires a PA after a fire, hurricane, water loss or other covered event to document the damage, build the claim, interpret the policy and negotiate with the carrier.</p>
<p>Because the PA is on the policyholder's side, the insurer does not pay them. The policyholder does, out of the settlement, under a written contract that spells out the fee percentage. Most states require a separate public adjuster license, often with a surety bond, and a few states do not allow public adjusting at all.</p>
<p>That arrangement shapes everything about the job. A PA who signs no clients earns nothing, and a PA who lands one large commercial fire may earn more from that single file than a staff adjuster makes in a month.</p>

<h2 id="contingency-fee-math">How the contingency fee math works</h2>
<p>The fee is a percentage of the amount the insurer pays. In many contracts it applies to the total settlement; some states restrict charging a fee on money the insurer had already paid or offered before the PA was hired. Here is how fees play out at common settlement sizes and percentages:</p>
<div class="table-wrap"><table class="data-table">
<thead><tr><th>Claim settlement</th><th>Fee at 10%</th><th>Fee at 15%</th><th>Fee at 20%</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>$20,000 (water damage)</td><td>$2,000</td><td>$3,000</td><td>$4,000</td></tr>
<tr><td>$50,000 (roof and interior)</td><td>$5,000</td><td>$7,500</td><td>$10,000</td></tr>
<tr><td>$120,000 (kitchen fire)</td><td>$12,000</td><td>$18,000</td><td>$24,000</td></tr>
<tr><td>$400,000 (major home fire)</td><td>$40,000</td><td>$60,000</td><td>$80,000</td></tr>
<tr><td>$1,000,000 (commercial loss)</td><td>$100,000</td><td>$150,000</td><td>$200,000</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table></div>
<p>These are gross fees before the PA's costs. If the PA works for a firm, the firm usually keeps a share and pays the adjuster a split. Marketing, travel, licensing, bonds, estimating software and sometimes outside experts such as engineers come out of what remains. Run your own scenarios with the <a href="/public-adjuster-fee-calculator/">public adjuster fee calculator</a>.</p>

<h3>A worked year</h3>
<p>Imagine a Texas PA working under the state's 10% cap who settles 30 residential claims in a year with an average settlement of $45,000. That is $1.35 million in recoveries and $135,000 in gross fees. If that PA works under a firm that keeps half, the adjuster's share is $67,500, which sits squarely in the typical range. A firm owner or solo PA keeps the whole fee but also carries all the overhead.</p>

<h2 id="state-fee-caps">State fee caps that limit a public adjuster salary</h2>
<p>Many states limit what PAs can charge, which directly caps earnings per file. Two of the biggest markets:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Texas:</strong> Public adjuster fees are capped at 10% of the settlement. Texas PAs make up for the lower cap with volume from hail, tornado and Gulf Coast hurricane claims.</li>
<li><strong>Florida:</strong> Fees are generally capped at 20%, but drop to 10% for claims tied to a declared state of emergency during the year after the declaration. Because so much Florida work follows hurricanes, a large share of PA fees there is earned at the lower rate.</li>
</ul>
<p>Other states set their own percentages, require specific contract language or cancellation periods, or limit fees on certain claim types. Always check the current rules with the state insurance department before signing clients. For broader pay comparisons, see our <a href="/salary-by-state/">insurance adjuster salary by state</a> estimates.</p>

<h2 id="texas-public-adjuster-pay">Public Adjuster Pay in Texas (10% Fee Cap)</h2>
<p>Texas is among the largest adjuster markets in the US, and it is a volume market for public adjusters. Every Texas PA needs a public adjuster license from the Texas Department of Insurance (TDI), and fees are capped at 10% of the settlement, so income depends on how many files you settle rather than on charging a higher percentage.</p>
<p>Here is what the 10% cap looks like on typical Texas claims:</p>
<div class="table-wrap"><table class="data-table">
<thead><tr><th>Texas claim example</th><th>Settlement</th><th>Maximum PA fee (10%)</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Dallas-Fort Worth hail roof and gutters</td><td>$30,000</td><td>$3,000</td></tr>
<tr><td>Austin burst-pipe water loss after a freeze</td><td>$45,000</td><td>$4,500</td></tr>
<tr><td>Houston hurricane wind and interior damage</td><td>$110,000</td><td>$11,000</td></tr>
<tr><td>Corpus Christi commercial hurricane loss</td><td>$600,000</td><td>$60,000</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table></div>
<p>The Texas storm calendar shapes the pipeline. Hail, wind and tornado claims peak from March through June across DFW, Central Texas and the Panhandle. Gulf hurricanes run June through November around Houston, Galveston, Corpus Christi and Beaumont. Winter freezes from December through February, like the February 2021 storm, can produce waves of burst-pipe claims, and West Texas and the Panhandle carry wildfire risk. For comparison, we estimate Texas staff adjusters earn a median of about $76,800. See our <a href="/insurance-adjuster-advice/insurance-adjuster-salary-texas/">insurance adjuster salary in Texas</a> guide for more.</p>

<h2 id="public-adjuster-salary-ranges">Public adjuster salary by career stage</h2>
<ol>
<li><strong>New PA at a firm (first 1-2 years):</strong> Often a modest base or draw plus a share of fees, or commission only. First-year income commonly lands below $50,000 while you learn estimating, policy language and negotiation.</li>
<li><strong>Experienced PA (3-7 years):</strong> Commonly $50,000 to $100,000, with a mix of firm-assigned files and self-generated clients.</li>
<li><strong>Established PA or firm owner:</strong> With a steady pipeline of commercial and large-loss claims, $150,000 or more is achievable. Owners also earn on the files their adjusters handle.</li>
</ol>
<p>Income is lumpy. Claims can take months to settle, and a PA might go several weeks without a check, then receive three large fees in one month.</p>

<h2 id="building-a-book">Building a book of business</h2>
<p>The single biggest driver of a public adjuster's income is client acquisition. Unlike staff adjusters, PAs do not have claims assigned to them by a carrier. Successful PAs typically build their pipeline through:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Referrals</strong> from contractors, restoration companies, plumbers, roofers and attorneys</li>
<li><strong>Commercial relationships</strong> with property managers, HOAs and business owners, where single losses are larger</li>
<li><strong>Past clients</strong> who return or refer neighbors after the next storm</li>
<li><strong>Storm response</strong> in areas hit by hurricanes or hail, within the solicitation rules each state imposes</li>
<li><strong>Online presence and reviews</strong>, which matter more every year as policyholders research before hiring</li>
</ul>
<p>Reputation compounds. Contractors refer PAs who settle claims fairly and quickly, and commercial clients stick with someone who has handled a large loss well.</p>

<div class="callout callout-tip"><strong>Pro tip:</strong> Many successful PAs spend a few years as staff or independent adjusters first. Learning how carriers evaluate claims, reserve files and use Xactimate makes you far more effective when you are negotiating from the other side of the table.</div>

<h2 id="pa-expenses">What comes out of a PA's fees</h2>
<p>Gross fees overstate take-home pay, especially for solo PAs and firm owners. Common costs include:</p>
<ul>
<li>State licenses, surety bonds and continuing education in every state you work</li>
<li>Errors and omissions insurance</li>
<li>An Xactimate subscription, photo and documentation tools, and a reliable vehicle</li>
<li>Marketing, a website, referral relationships and storm-response travel</li>
<li>Outside experts on disputed claims, such as engineers, contents specialists or forensic accountants</li>
<li>Self-employment tax and quarterly estimated payments if you are not on a firm's payroll</li>
</ul>
<p>Cash flow is the other hidden cost. Because a fee is only earned when the insurer pays, a PA can spend months working a contested commercial claim before seeing a dollar. Keeping several months of expenses in reserve is standard advice for anyone going solo.</p>

<h2 id="pa-vs-other-adjusters">Public adjusters vs staff and independent adjusters</h2>
<p>A staff adjuster trades upside for stability: a salary near the BLS median of $76,790, benefits and claims delivered to their desk. An independent adjuster earns per claim from IA firms and rides the storm cycle. A public adjuster has the most entrepreneurial role of the three, with income tied directly to sales and settlement results. Our comparison of <a href="/insurance-adjuster-advice/how-much-do-insurance-adjusters-make/">how much insurance adjusters make</a> lays the three side by side.</p>

<h2 id="getting-licensed">Getting started as a public adjuster</h2>
<p>Most states require a dedicated public adjuster license, which usually involves a pre-licensing course, a state exam, fingerprinting and a surety bond. Some states also require prior adjusting experience or sponsorship. Plenty of PAs begin with a company or independent adjuster license to learn the trade. In Texas, that often means starting with the TDI all-lines adjuster license, the most popular designated home state license nationally because of its broad reciprocity, before applying for the separate Texas public adjuster license. TDI-approved certification courses can satisfy the all-lines exam requirement. An online course at <a href="https://www.adjustingonline.com/courses/" rel="sponsored noopener" target="_blank">AdjustingOnline</a> is one way to prepare. Use <strong>joe20</strong> for 20% off. Visit our <a href="/">insurance adjuster salary</a> hub for more career paths.</p>
